WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.368
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.368 --> 00:00:03.869
is a very talented writer,
actor, and director

00:00:03.869 --> 00:00:05.004
whose film "Belfast"

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:07.540
is nominated
for seven Academy Awards.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.043
It's currently playing in
select theaters nationwide

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:12.278
and available to stream on
Apple TV+

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:13.613
and Amazon Prime Video.

00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:15.048
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:18.785
-Every night before I go to
sleep, when I say my prayers,

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:23.056
I ask God if he'd fix it so that
when I wake up in the morning,

00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:25.525
I'm the best footballer
in the world.

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:27.594
Then I also ask him as well

00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:30.496
that when I grow up,
can I marry Catherine?

00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:32.165
Even if she loves Ronnie Boyd.

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:34.767
But she could still see him,
but she'd marry me.

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:36.369
That's what I want.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:39.439
-Please welcome to the show
Kenneth Branagh, everybody.

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:41.841
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:43.376
-Hiya.

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:44.543
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:47.513
-Kenneth, congratulations.

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:50.082
Seven Oscar nominations.
-Thank you, Seth.

00:00:50.082 --> 00:00:51.717
-That must feel very special.

00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:54.387
It was amazing.
It still feels amazing.

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.522
And it was an incredible
morning, actually.

00:00:56.522 --> 00:00:57.723
We have four producers.

00:00:57.723 --> 00:01:00.125
Two of us were in London.
Two of us were in L.A.

00:01:00.125 --> 00:01:02.428
We were both listening to
the announcements live,

00:01:02.428 --> 00:01:04.229
but there was a sort of time
delay which meant that,

00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:07.433
bizarrely, we got the
information just a few seconds

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:09.502
ahead in London
of them in L.A.

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:12.272
So they were watching us,
because we had each of us muted

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:13.772
so we could
hear the announcements.

00:01:13.772 --> 00:01:15.475
And they were seeing
body language going,

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:17.277
"No, we didn't get it --
Oh, yes!"

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:19.912
Just ahead, and not quite
understanding what it was.

00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:21.614
So they died a thousand deaths

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:23.949
while we got the information
a bit quicker,

00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:26.518
even though they were in
the City of the Angels itself.

00:01:26.518 --> 00:01:28.354
But still, it was an amazing --

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:32.825
it's hard to describe the kind
of champagne moment that it is.

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:34.627
You don't really believe
it's quite happening.

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:37.630
And then suddenly, anybody
you've ever met in your life

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.033
is in touch with you
for the next three hours.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.701
-You know, very early in
your career, with "Henry V,"

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:43.870
you were Oscar nominated.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.504
Do you have a completely
different perspective

00:01:45.504 --> 00:01:47.774
going into the night
than you had back then,

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:50.610
when you were just getting
started as a film director?

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.946
-It's still pretty amazing.

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:54.213
It's so unusual.

00:01:54.213 --> 00:01:56.048
I mean, this film, "Belfast,"
is about

00:01:56.048 --> 00:01:59.018
a boy from a working-class
family in North Belfast

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:00.786
who loved going to the movies

00:02:00.786 --> 00:02:03.189
and saw all these kind of
gifts from Hollywood.

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:06.091
So, to make a film
that's in Hollywood

00:02:06.091 --> 00:02:08.227
attracting people to the movies,
and you realize,

00:02:08.227 --> 00:02:10.997
"Hey, the kid from the
working-class Belfast family

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:13.332
is me, and this is happening."

00:02:13.332 --> 00:02:15.969
Frankly, Seth, I still haven't
got my head around it.

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.938
It was bizarre enough that I was

00:02:18.938 --> 00:02:22.174
doing Shakespeare as
a 29-year-old there.

00:02:22.174 --> 00:02:24.577
So to be here and telling
a personal story

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:26.379
and having it touch people,
that's --

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:27.746
I mean, it's a mind-blower.

00:02:27.746 --> 00:02:29.015
-So, this is about --

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:30.349
you know,
it's based on your life.

00:02:30.349 --> 00:02:31.885
It's based on growing up
in Belfast.

00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:33.786
And I assume usually you have to

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:35.455
do a great deal of research
about a film,

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:37.089
especially
a period piece like this.

00:02:37.089 --> 00:02:38.657
But because it was
your childhood,

00:02:38.657 --> 00:02:40.826
did you have to do
the same level of research,

00:02:40.826 --> 00:02:43.296
or did you just
count on your own memory?

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:46.332
-I decided that I wouldn't ask,
because I don't know about you,

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:48.033
but I find it's
pretty hard to achieve

00:02:48.033 --> 00:02:49.802
objective truth about anything.

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:51.571
I often find that
with my wife and I,

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:52.838
there may be a dispute about

00:02:52.838 --> 00:02:54.207
literally what happened
in the morning.

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:56.275
It's probably not the same
in your household, but we --

00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:58.511
you know, we can't agree about,
"we had coffee."

00:02:58.511 --> 00:02:59.779
"We didn't have coffee."

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:01.080
"You said that thing.
You said you would do it."

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.081
"No, I didn't.
You said you would do it."

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:03.349
"That was yesterday." Anyway.

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.517
That kind of thing,
in the course of 10 hours,

00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:06.985
seems to happen regularly.

00:03:06.985 --> 00:03:08.988
So I thought, I'm not
going to ask my family,

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:10.790
and then I'll show it to them
when I'm finished.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.459
And I did, and my brother,
who was part of the whole story,

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:14.860
then read it and he loved it.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:15.861
He was very emotional.

00:03:15.861 --> 00:03:16.963
He said,
"But you didn't do that.

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:19.765
I did that, and you did that."

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:22.068
So, it was a whole kind of --
I was glad I waited.

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:23.436
[ Laughter ]

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:25.671
-And can we expect that
your family

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:27.473
will make a rebuttal
"Belfast" film

00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:30.176
where they tell it
the way it actually happened?

00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:32.778
-Yeah. Yeah, I would think so.

00:03:32.778 --> 00:03:35.981
Yeah, I think put
your finger on it there,

00:03:35.981 --> 00:03:37.350
because yeah.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:38.785
Inevitably, you just decided,

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:41.220
this is going to go through
the eyes of a 9-year-old boy,

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.856
And it's also a 9-year-old boy
who loves movies.

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:46.692
So because, you know,
I saw so many Westerns,

00:03:46.692 --> 00:03:49.094
particularly from this country,
a lot of the story

00:03:49.094 --> 00:03:50.863
goes through that kind of lens.

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:53.066
You see people as the good guys
and the bad guys,

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:55.501
and the street
becomes your Wild West town.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.403
And I guess, you know,
my brother

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:59.271
definitely saw it
in different terms,

00:03:59.271 --> 00:04:01.373
but he was five years older
and he knew everything.

00:04:01.373 --> 00:04:03.509
And he still knows everything,
and I still love him.

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:05.377
-Do you, historically,
with your work,

00:04:05.377 --> 00:04:08.748
have you shown it to family,
sort of early drafts?

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:10.550
-I don't know about you,
I find that

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.851
when I show people things
that are work in progress,

00:04:12.851 --> 00:04:15.521
it doesn't matter what you say,

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:16.922
and they'll claim
they understand it --

00:04:16.922 --> 00:04:19.125
so, you'll say to somebody that
you know and love, say,

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:20.760
"So, this is
an early cut of a film.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.762
It's a special effects film,
so there'll be

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:24.664
some flying machines
and all the rest of it,

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:26.598
so there's some green screen
or some blue screen.

00:04:26.598 --> 00:04:28.200
So just bear that in mind.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.036
There's some green screen
and some blue screen,

00:04:30.036 --> 00:04:33.138
so bear in mind, you'll see
some green and some blue."

00:04:33.138 --> 00:04:34.607
"Oh, don't, listen,
I understand.

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:36.075
I see, I know how to
watch a movie."

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.843
The lights go up
and they turn around and,

00:04:37.843 --> 00:04:40.213
"You know, there's a lot of
green screen in that film.

00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:42.014
You know, I actually,
I don't know if you saw.

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:43.349
I saw a lot of blue.

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:45.150
A lot of blue, actually,
in addition to the green.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.185
There's green and blue."

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:49.856
"Is there? Ya don't say!
I told you!"

00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:52.825
So, I tend now to
only show people

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:54.827
things when they're
pretty blooming far on.

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:56.529
-I think that's a good
lesson to have learned.

00:04:56.529 --> 00:04:59.532
This isn't just up against
your family's memories.

00:04:59.532 --> 00:05:01.734
This is about the street
you grew up in.

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:05.304
So I imagine you have neighbors
from that time period

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:08.040
who it also, you know,
personally affected

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.975
their memory of the place.

00:05:09.975 --> 00:05:13.446
-Yeah, and it's been wonderful
that since the film's come out,

00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:16.115
several people who lived in
that street have been in touch.

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:18.851
You know, we lived
next to a park.

00:05:18.851 --> 00:05:21.520
There were railings that were
bent sideways

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.523
so that people
my size could squeeze through.

00:05:23.523 --> 00:05:25.624
Several people who claim to
be the person

00:05:25.624 --> 00:05:28.493
whose unique strength
opened that bendy bar,

00:05:28.493 --> 00:05:29.662
as we used to call it.

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:32.331
The Charles Atlases
of the time have said,

00:05:32.331 --> 00:05:34.100
"It was me, it was me,
it was me."

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.534
But a lot of people
have really said

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:36.935
how right they think
the film got it.

00:05:36.935 --> 00:05:38.237
It's how they remember it.

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:40.272
But they reminded me of things
that I did not know.

00:05:40.272 --> 00:05:42.474
If any of your viewers know what
the following explains,

00:05:42.474 --> 00:05:43.676
please tell me.

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:45.844
So, a friend of mine
from the street,

00:05:45.844 --> 00:05:48.714
wrote to me recently and said,
"In the summer," he said,

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:50.683
"your hair was always greasy."

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:51.851
And I said, "Why?"

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:53.419
He said because your mother,
in the summer,

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:56.689
as a matter of course,
would cover it with butter.

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:58.490
I don't know whether

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.593
she intended to put me
in a bread roll or something,

00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:01.927
I don't know, as a punishment.

00:06:01.927 --> 00:06:04.963
But it may have been to do
with conditioning my hair,

00:06:04.963 --> 00:06:06.399
which, you know,
is largely still here,

00:06:06.399 --> 00:06:07.967
so maybe I should thank her.

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:10.869
But little details like
that were lovely to hear

00:06:10.869 --> 00:06:12.238
from people
who were really there.

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:13.772
-I feel like you -- I mean,

00:06:13.772 --> 00:06:15.474
if the butter industry
is hearing this,

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:17.610
they should really
hire you as a spokesperson.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.146
I mean, that head of hair,
you with some butter,

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:21.414
saying, "This is the secret."

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:22.882
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:25.251
-Listen, I think you've got a
fine head of hair there, Seth.

00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:27.820
Have you secretly
been using butter?

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:30.489
-I don't like to say it, but
I have been buttering my hair

00:06:30.489 --> 00:06:31.858
since I was a kid, yeah.

00:06:31.858 --> 00:06:34.993
[ Laughter ]
-We have very similar mothers.

00:06:34.993 --> 00:06:37.029
-I Can't Believe
It's Not Butter!

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:40.633
-So, Dame Judi Dench is
fantastic in this film.

00:06:40.633 --> 00:06:43.702
And she was here recently
and talked about,

00:06:43.702 --> 00:06:47.072
you guys have a very long
history of working together.

00:06:47.072 --> 00:06:48.807
She has directed you onstage.

00:06:48.807 --> 00:06:50.576
You obviously directed her now
in this film.

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:52.077
And she told a story about you

00:06:52.077 --> 00:06:55.047
getting kicked out of a studio,
a television studio,

00:06:55.047 --> 00:07:00.819
for laughing during a production
of Ibsen's "Ghosts,"

00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:02.254
which is not known as a comedy.

00:07:02.254 --> 00:07:03.856
What exactly happened?

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:07.460
-Well, if, like Judi,
you are a genius

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.595
and when you are playing
these dark roles

00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:12.664
and you inhabit all this sort of
sturm-and-drang woman

00:07:12.664 --> 00:07:15.835
whose father and husband
have ruined her life

00:07:15.835 --> 00:07:17.937
and now her son has
inherited this terrible disease,

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:19.839
and, you know,
life is in a field

00:07:19.839 --> 00:07:22.207
where the sun only goes across
for a narrow part of the day

00:07:22.207 --> 00:07:23.976
in the summer
and everything's pretty gloomy.

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:26.111
When you inhabit that
with that intensity,

00:07:26.111 --> 00:07:27.979
I think it makes you
very sort of giddy.

00:07:27.979 --> 00:07:29.281
So when you have to do --

00:07:29.281 --> 00:07:31.450
so, you've done the big,
you know, fancy scenes.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:34.586
Then we had a scene where all
we had to do was eat dinner.

00:07:34.586 --> 00:07:36.155
The late, great
Natasha Richardson

00:07:36.155 --> 00:07:37.957
delivers some potatoes
to myself,

00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:41.927
to the great Michael Gambon,
and then to Judi.

00:07:41.927 --> 00:07:45.364
When Judi had been passed by
Natasha Richardson,

00:07:45.364 --> 00:07:48.667
the potatoes had been dumped,
and then Michael Gambon

00:07:48.667 --> 00:07:51.136
was invited to join in,
and Natasha said,

00:07:51.136 --> 00:07:52.638
"How many potatoes
can I give you?"

00:07:52.638 --> 00:07:55.107
And he said "17."

00:07:55.107 --> 00:07:57.776
Judi Dench then
literally fell forward.

00:07:57.776 --> 00:08:00.445
So her head, the dame of
the the British empires' face

00:08:00.445 --> 00:08:04.951
is now in the potatoes, and all
she's doing is laughing.

00:08:04.951 --> 00:08:07.119
Gambon, because the camera's
gone past him,

00:08:07.119 --> 00:08:10.656
slides underneath, and now it
resides on a close-up of me.

00:08:10.656 --> 00:08:13.092
And Natasha Richardson is
holding the plate

00:08:13.092 --> 00:08:15.527
with the potatoes,
and the spoon is rattling,

00:08:15.527 --> 00:08:16.929
because she's laughing so much.

00:08:16.929 --> 00:08:20.366
At which point, a voice comes
over saying, "Stop!

00:08:20.366 --> 00:08:22.868
Stop! You know who you are!

00:08:22.868 --> 00:08:26.939
Get out of the studio, now,
Ms. Dench and Mr. Branagh,

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:28.140
and do not return!"

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:30.476
So it was her fault.
It was her fault.

00:08:30.476 --> 00:08:32.478
Plus, she ruined all her makeup,
which I didn't.

00:08:32.478 --> 00:08:35.213
-I'm very glad you cleaned
this up for everyone.

00:08:35.213 --> 00:08:39.518
The last thing I want to ask,
this is a movie that,

00:08:39.518 --> 00:08:41.853
obviously this was a time
of great violence in Belfast,

00:08:41.853 --> 00:08:45.524
and yet there's a great amount
of humor in the film.

00:08:45.524 --> 00:08:47.960
I would imagine that must be
a part you remember

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:50.529
of your upbringing, that even
in a very troubled time,

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:53.366
there was a great amount
of laughter amongst your family.

00:08:53.366 --> 00:08:55.935
amongst that community,
that neighborhood.

00:08:55.935 --> 00:08:59.538
-The quickest way to a joke
in dark times

00:08:59.538 --> 00:09:01.106
was how you got over it.

00:09:01.106 --> 00:09:02.240
There's a scene at the end

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:04.143
where a much-loved character
passes away,

00:09:04.143 --> 00:09:06.878
when my father was involved
with that moment in real life,

00:09:06.878 --> 00:09:09.381
he had to share
a downstairs room

00:09:09.381 --> 00:09:11.650
with an open casket and a body,

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:14.987
and five days' worth of
the locals coming

00:09:14.987 --> 00:09:17.623
and trying to get back,
in terms of drink,

00:09:17.623 --> 00:09:19.991
what they felt
they had been owed

00:09:19.991 --> 00:09:21.860
by the person who had departed.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.330
It's very intense.
It's dark. It's mad.

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:26.432
People drink a lot,
but by the end of it,

00:09:26.432 --> 00:09:28.967
you then, after all that keening
that the Irish do --

00:09:28.967 --> 00:09:32.571
they own their grief --
suddenly, Jamie Dornan comes on,

00:09:32.571 --> 00:09:34.739
who I think you look
very like, by the way.

00:09:34.739 --> 00:09:35.974
-Thank you very much.

00:09:35.974 --> 00:09:37.109
And you're a director.

00:09:37.109 --> 00:09:38.277
You have an eye for
this kind of thing.

00:09:38.277 --> 00:09:39.712
-Exactly.

00:09:39.712 --> 00:09:41.913
Tell your wife it was literally,
you were down to the last two.

00:09:41.913 --> 00:09:43.082
It was you and Jamie,

00:09:43.082 --> 00:09:44.783
and the only reason
he got it, Seth, was that

00:09:44.783 --> 00:09:47.987
he did this fantastic version of
"Everlasting Love" at the wake,

00:09:47.987 --> 00:09:51.757
which allowed everybody to get
out of this dark, grieving scene

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:54.393
into a big, mad, knees-up,
singing scene.

00:09:54.393 --> 00:09:56.695
When you have that in
your repertoire,

00:09:56.695 --> 00:09:58.564
I think Jamie Dornan
is out the window,

00:09:58.564 --> 00:10:00.132
and your wife will renew

00:10:00.132 --> 00:10:02.801
her keen interest in
your romantic possibilities.

00:10:02.801 --> 00:10:05.338
-Well, you have really
sent me off with

00:10:05.338 --> 00:10:07.405
a sort of, I don't know,
esteem-building

00:10:07.405 --> 00:10:09.942
I don't get from a lot my
guests, so thank you so much.

00:10:09.942 --> 00:10:11.410
Congratulations on the film.

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:12.478
Good luck at the Oscars.

00:10:12.478 --> 00:10:14.113
Kenneth Branagh, everybody!

00:10:14.113 --> 00:10:15.414
-Thank you.

00:10:15.414 --> 00:10:18.317
-"Belfast" is playing in
select theaters nationwide,

00:10:18.317 --> 00:10:20.152
and available to stream
on Apple TV+

00:10:20.152 --> 00:10:21.152
and Amazon Prime Video.

00:10:21.152 --> 00:10:22.088
We will be right back!

